in 1982 Jim Henson proved he was more  than just the Muppet guy with an epic  fantasy about the struggle for power and  survival in a fantastical land decades  later the story is continuing with a  Netflix miniseries so there's no better  time to delve into the untold truths of  the Dark Crystal perhaps the least  popular sketch in Saturday Night Live  history is the season one recurring bit  the land of Gorch  it featured fantastical and grotesque  monster creations with names like spread  and ploobis although it stemmed from the  minds of Jim Henson and company it  lacked that signature Muppet magic after  one year all parties involved decided it  would be best to end the land of Gorch  while Gorch wound up a bizarre footnote  in SNL lore it directly inspired one of  Henson's most triumphant works in the  70s Henson developed a deep appreciation  for the work of fantasy illustrator  Brian Froud an English artist with an  affinity for bringing exquisitely  detailed fantasy beings to the page  around the time that Henson got into  frowns work Froude was thinking about  turning his creatures into a puppet  based film so he happily accepted a 1977  invite from Henson to visit his workshop  in England once roud saw the highly  detailed land of Gorch puppets  he knew Henson was the man to bring his  illustrations to life and the two  creators agreed to make a fantasy film  together the Dark Crystal is one of the  first live-action narrative films with  no visible humans in the cast it's also  one of the few in which characters speak  languages invented by linguists  specifically for the project children's  fantasy author Alan garner provided some  helpful suggestions for the Dark Crystal  team based on some ancient languages for  example a language created for the  Skeksis was based on an offshoot of  ancient Egyptian the one spoken by the  pod people is a take on serbo-croatian  spoken in parts of the former Yugoslavia  producer Gary Kurtz has noted that  people who are fluent in Polish Russian  and other Eastern European languages say  that they can recognize some of the  words for the sake of realism the Dark  Crystal filmmakers opted not to create a  new language whole cloth from scratch as  Kurtz explained to Starlog magazine  certainly it's much easier to make up  nonsense sounds but you can sense if it  doesn't have any basis in reality in a  real language there are certain  repeatable phrases and a structure which  makes the sounds mean something the Dark  Crystal held its first test screening in  Washington DC in March 1982 Jim Henson  figured the plot would be clear enough  through the characters actions and tone  but the test audience didn't think so  and neither did executives overseeing  the project they thought they had an  expensive flop on their hands and that  they'd spent 25 million dollars on a  baffling impenetrable experimental film  Henson had no choice but to follow the  directives of the people in charge and  dub the movie into English screenwriter  David Odell had to essentially compose a  whole new script with dialogue that not  only told the story but matched up to  the puppets mouth movements then Henson  had to hire a whole new cast of voice  actors then have editors sync their  words with the finished visuals in total  it took four months to make the dark  crystals audio track workable and  creating a vast and elaborate fantasy  world on par with Westeros or  middle-earth Jim Henson and his  collaborators developed a wealth of  background stories and other ideas for  the Dark Crystal there have been many  video games comics and books continuing  the story of the gelfling z' and the  Skeksis but a follow-up film never  materialized in 2006 the Jim Henson  Company announced the beginning of  production on power of the Dark Crystal  a sequel set centuries after the first  film it was to follow a girl made of  fire who teams up with a rogue gelfling  to swipe a shard of the crystal to  reignite a dying Sun producer Lisa  Henson told MTV that it was based on the  sequel idea discussed two decades  earlier by Jim Henson  Dark Crystal screenwriter David Odell  Samurai Jack creator genndy tartakovsky  signed on to direct the sequel which  would have combined computer-generated  animation with live-action animatronics  but alas it wasn't meant to be  in 2012 tart offski told indieWIRE we  did visual designs we did a script we  started testing things we were always a  couple of dollars short and it just kind  of fell apart in August 20 1960 viewed  one of the most hotly anticipated and  long-awaited projects in recent memory  the Dark Crystal Age of resistance a  prequel miniseries to the Dark Crystal  it all started to come together around  2011 when Clash of the Titans director  Louis Leterrier took some meetings with  the Henson Company he had watched the  Dark Crystal on the Titan set constantly  for inspiration prompting the company to  ask him to helm its Dark Crystal sequel  project he was excited about what  happened before the events of the film  prompting the company to change course  and make a prequel miniseries instead of  a sequel film filmmaking technology has  significantly progressed in the past few  decades  solet Rea had many more tools at his  disposal when making age of resistance  than Jim Henson did back in 1982 but  Letarte EA is such a super fan of the  original film that he vowed to use  practical effects as much as possible  the canvas is much bigger but the  feeling is the same while some CGI  technology was implemented it was only  to digitally remove puppeteers from  footage of action-heavy scenes but  there's still a lot that can be  accomplished with puppetry an age of  resistance is just one indicator of the  dark crystals long legacy and now the  prophecy is fulfilled check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  stuff are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and hit the belt so you  don't miss a single one  you  [Music]
 

